<p>A federal jury in Miami convicted Arcangel Pretel Ortiz, Antonio Intriago, Walter Veintemilla and James Solages over the 2021 assassination of Haitian President Jovenel Moïse, who was fatally shot at his home near Port-au-Prince on July 7, 2021. NBC Miami reported jurors found the men guilty of conspiring to kill or kidnap Moïse, providing material support for the plot and violating the U.S. Neutrality Act. Prosecutors said South Florida was a planning and financing hub for a scheme to recruit former Colombian soldiers and provide money, weapons, ammunition and tactical gear for the operation. Moïse, 53, was killed in an early-morning attack, and his wife, Martine Moïse, was wounded and flown to the United States for treatment. The New York Times reported that the conspiracy convictions did not reveal who ordered the assassination.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/n8N-mHV8gvBGepz8Rg6wabvC6dxNWAOXhUGAp-tkRAzXgYfnwIruClxzRDrduqIDXGBYmarDoPmrwRMc5gkGb0CV-rNr2-NcK7nEADfkHMXSAQHoeL-nw3pY-lcUBkdrrSYOdAWJA8_tGE4AgeFciTYNre_w-tZhzTwJ3ozzpnDnKuT63r4k-xx4cYIy8fSDrpGCKqyLefMyKw' alt='A mural of Jovenel Moïse near his home in Port-au-Prince, Haiti, where he was assassinated in 2021.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Corporate links: Intriago and Pretel Ortiz were principals of South Florida security companies known as CTU, while Veintemilla was tied to Worldwide Capital Lending Group.</li><li>Trial record: The verdict came after 39 days of hearings before U.S. District Judge Jacqueline Becerra, according to RCC Noticias.</li><li>Prior convictions: RCC Noticias reported that nine people have now been convicted in the United States over the assassination, including five previously sentenced to life in prison.</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/8/four-convicted-in-us-related-to-killing-of-haitian-president-jovenel-moise'>Four convicted in US related to killing of Haitian President Jovenel Moise</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><